{"type":"lesson.started","request_id":"ea9b94ab-6719-48f2-95e0-177bdf8f1660","title":"Projectile motion: horizontal and vertical velocity"}
{"type":"lesson.step","request_id":"ea9b94ab-6719-48f2-95e0-177bdf8f1660","step":{"id":"s1","script":"Ignoring air resistance, a projectile follows a curved path. Its velocity has independent horizontal and vertical components. Here, take gravity as ten meters per second squared.","ops":[{"op":"text","id":"title","region":"A1","content":"Projectile velocity"},{"op":"equation","id":"initial","anchor":{"el":"title","side":"below","gap":0.08},"latex":"v_{0x}=4\\,\\mathrm{m/s},\\quad v_{0y}=6\\,\\mathrm{m/s}"},{"op":"figure","id":"flight","region":"right","frame":{"x":[-0.5,5.5],"y":[-0.6,3.2],"axes":"none"},"marks":[{"kind":"segment","id":"ground","from":[-0.3,0],"to":[5.2,0]},{"kind":"param","id":"path","x":"4*t","y":"6*t-5*t^2","t":[0,1.2]},{"kind":"point","id":"launch","at":{"on":"path","t":0},"label":"launch"},{"kind":"point","id":"apex","at":{"on":"path","t":0.5},"label":"top"},{"kind":"point","id":"fall","at":{"on":"path","t":0.8333333333333334}}]}],"checkpoint":null}}
{"type":"lesson.step","request_id":"ea9b94ab-6719-48f2-95e0-177bdf8f1660","step":{"id":"s2","script":"With no horizontal force, horizontal acceleration is zero. These equal arrows show the same horizontal velocity throughout the flight.","ops":[{"op":"figure","id":"horizontal","on":"flight","marks":[{"kind":"segment","id":"vxstart","from":{"at":"launch"},"to":[0.8,0],"arrow":true,"label":"4 m/s"},{"kind":"segment","id":"vxtop","from":{"at":"apex"},"to":[3.2,1.8],"arrow":true,"label":"4 m/s"},{"kind":"segment","id":"vxfall","from":{"at":"fall"},"to":[4.8,1],"arrow":true,"label":"4 m/s"}]},{"op":"equation","id":"horizontal_law","anchor":{"el":"initial","side":"below","gap":0.1},"latex":"a_x=0\\quad\\Rightarrow\\quad v_x=4\\,\\mathrm{m/s}"}],"checkpoint":null}}
{"type":"lesson.step","request_id":"ea9b94ab-6719-48f2-95e0-177bdf8f1660","step":{"id":"s3","script":"Gravity changes only vertical velocity: upward initially, zero at the top, then downward. Zero vertical velocity does not mean the projectile stops.","ops":[{"op":"figure","id":"vertical","on":"flight","marks":[{"kind":"segment","id":"vystart","from":{"at":"launch"},"to":[0,1.2],"arrow":true,"label":"+6 m/s"},{"kind":"segment","id":"vyfall","from":{"at":"fall"},"to":[4,0.2],"arrow":true,"label":"−4 m/s"},{"kind":"text","at":[2.4,2.2],"text":"vᵧ = 0"}]},{"op":"equation","id":"vertical_law","anchor":{"el":"horizontal_law","side":"below","gap":0.1},"latex":"a_y=-g,\\qquad v_y=6-10t\\quad(\\mathrm{m/s})"}],"checkpoint":{"question":"At the highest point, what are the horizontal and vertical velocities?","expected_gist":"Horizontal: 4 m/s; vertical: zero."}}}
{"type":"lesson.step","request_id":"ea9b94ab-6719-48f2-95e0-177bdf8f1660","step":{"id":"s4","script":"At one second, combine the rightward and downward components. The resulting velocity points tangent to the path; its magnitude is the speed.","ops":[{"op":"figure","id":"resultant","on":"flight","marks":[{"kind":"segment","from":[4.8,1],"to":[4.8,0.2],"stroke":"dashed"},{"kind":"segment","from":[4,0.2],"to":[4.8,0.2],"stroke":"dashed"},{"kind":"segment","id":"velocity","from":{"at":"fall"},"to":[4.8,0.2],"arrow":true,"label":"v"},{"kind":"text","at":[3.6,2.6],"text":"t = 1 s"}]},{"op":"equation","id":"speed","anchor":{"el":"vertical_law","side":"below","gap":0.1},"latex":"|\\mathbf v|=\\sqrt{v_x^2+v_y^2}=\\sqrt{4^2+(-4)^2}\\approx5.7\\,\\mathrm{m/s}"}],"checkpoint":null}}
